# Lands at: /etc/udev/rules.d/10-voyager-gtx-trim.rules   (mode 0644)
#
# CARRIED OVER UNCHANGED from the Ventoy-era install. The rule matches on USB
# vendor/product (1b1c:1a0e) and on nothing about the partition table, the
# filesystem or the bootloader, so nothing in the falcon move touches it.
#
# One consequence of the new layout worth stating rather than discovering: the
# oversized-discard hazard the clamp exists for has NOT gone away. It was the
# exFAT partition that generated 4 GiB UNMAP requests, and the new p2 is still
# exFAT and still has tens of gigabytes of contiguous free space. Same
# filesystem, same failure, same clamp.
#
# Corsair Flash Voyager GTX (1b1c:1a0e): SandForce SF-2281 SSD behind a
# USB-SATA bridge. The bridge reports lbpme=0 in READ CAPACITY(16) while its
# Logical Block Provisioning VPD advertises LBPU=1 with a Maximum unmap LBA
# count of 4194240, so Linux declines to enable discard on its own. UNMAP was
# verified working by hand: a real discard returned Good status and the range
# read back as zeros.
#
# Assignment order is load-bearing. Writing provisioning_mode recomputes the
# queue limits, and because lbpme=0 the sd driver never copied max_unmap_blocks
# out of the VPD, so it falls back to SD_MAX_WS16_BLOCKS (0x7FFFFF) -- exactly
# twice what this device accepts. ext4's fstrim never issues anything that large
# (128 MiB per block group), but exFAT does: the ISO partition's contiguous
# free space generates 4 GiB requests, which earn an ILLEGAL REQUEST and latch
# provisioning_mode to "disabled" device-wide. The clamp must therefore follow
# the mode, never precede it.
#
# manage_shutdown=1 makes the sd driver send START STOP UNIT before power-off,
# which a SAT bridge translates to STANDBY IMMEDIATE, telling the drive to
# commit its FTL mapping table instead of finding out when VBUS drops. The
# kernel defaults this off for USB because some bridges stop responding until
# re-enumerated; verified safe here across both a cold poweroff and a warm
# reboot on 2026-09-07. manage_system_start_stop is deliberately left at 0: it
# would also apply to suspend, putting the root filesystem at risk on resume for
# no gain at shutdown.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="sd*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1b1c", ATTRS{idProduct}=="1a0e", ATTR{device/scsi_disk/*/provisioning_mode}="unmap", ATTR{queue/discard_max_bytes}="2147450880", ATTR{device/scsi_disk/*/manage_shutdown}="1"
